  "content": {
    "id": "p64-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Use the quadratic formula to solve for $x$, expressing your answers in exact form.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p64-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(x-1)(x^2-2x-4)=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=1,\\ 1\\pm\\sqrt5$",
          "worked": "By the null factor law, $x=1$ or $x^2-2x-4=0$. For the quadratic:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{-(-2)\\pm\\sqrt{(-2)^2-4(1)(-4)}}{2(1)}\\\\x&=\\frac{2\\pm\\sqrt{20}}2\\\\x&=1\\pm\\sqrt5\\end{aligned}$$\nHence $x=1,\\ 1+\\sqrt5,\\ 1-\\sqrt5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      },
      {
        "id": "p64-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$(x+2)(x^2+6x+10)=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=-2$ only.",
          "worked": "By the null factor law, $x=-2$ or $x^2+6x+10=0$. For the quadratic:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=\\frac{-6\\pm\\sqrt{6^2-4(1)(10)}}{2(1)}\\\\x&=\\frac{-6\\pm\\sqrt{-4}}2\\end{aligned}$$\nThe quadratic has no real solutions because its discriminant is negative. Hence the only real solution is $x=-2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
  },
